        "body_paragraph_zero_based": 4974,
        "direct_body_paragraph_zero_based": 2139,
        "paragraph_id": "4B3DE571",
        "text": "Thus combining (B.64), (B.65) and (B.66) explicit expressions for  and  are obtained. The predicted values are again corrected using a variant of the McCormack scheme and including the pressure difference implicitly gives the corrector step:"
      }
    ],
    "following_nonempty_paragraphs": [
      {
        "container_path": "models/XBeach/raw/source_code/trunk/doc/manual/XBeach_manual_kingsday.docx",
        "member": "word/document.xml",
        "start_xml_ordinal": 104738,
        "body_paragraph_zero_based": 4978,
        "direct_body_paragraph_zero_based": 2143,
        "paragraph_id": "0DF836AF",
        "text": "Where  and  are obtained using relations similar to (B.61). Note that similar to (B.64) and again the kinematic boundary conditions is substituted for ."
      },
      {
        "container_path": "models/XBeach/raw/source_code/trunk/doc/manual/XBeach_manual_kingsday.docx",
        "member": "word/document.xml",
        "start_xml_ordinal": 104913,
        "body_paragraph_zero_based": 4980,
        "direct_body_paragraph_zero_based": 2145,
        "paragraph_id": "40B9C80D",
        "text": "The discrete vertical momentum balance of (B.63) and (B.67) looks very different from the relations found in Zijlema and Stelling (2005), Zijlema and Stelling (2008)  and Smit (2008). This is mainly due to the application of the McCormack scheme for the advection. The discretization of the pressure term is numerically fully equivalent to either the Keller box scheme as used in Zijlema and Stelling (2005), Zijlema and Stelling (2008) or the Hermetian relation used in Smit (2008)."
      }
    ],
